title: c1.1 Mindful Walk
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Indoors, Pepper; outdoors, a pocket guide with the same voice. Five selected: Pepper as indoor host (TECH-01), the pocket guide (TECH-02), garden beacons (TECH-03), staff notification and session record (TECH-04), listening library and recommender (TECH-05). Four rejected, kept as Foundation knowledge: GPS tracker (TECH-06), Pepper on the walk (TECH-07), headphones (TECH-08), stress wristband (TECH-09). No hands-on testing yet; hardware details from vendor documentation.

= Technology Option - Pepper as indoor host (selected) [TECH-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-01
|Name|Pepper as indoor host (selected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Vendor documentation; the course platform. Pepper has been used as a mindfulness coach with healthy adults (Bodala et al., 2021; Alimardani et al., 2020). No hands-on testing by this project.
|Category|Social Robot
|Capabilities|(((
Humanoid, about 1.2 m, wheeled base. Speech, chest tablet, touch sensors, eye and shoulder LEDs.

* Invitation at the door before the walk (F1); welcome back after it
* Seated practice in the lounge or by the window (F3)
* Breathing light: LEDs fade in and out at a slow breathing pace - an anchor without words
* Listening host in the lounge (F6)
)))
|Limitations|(((
* Flat indoor floors only: no grass, gravel, kerbs or slopes (vendor guidance; check against the course unit)
* Not waterproof
* Size: may not fit beside a bed; not used in bedrooms (Open item O-9)
* Humanoid form: may read as supervisor (HFC-08)
* Cameras present even when switched off
* Speech recognition of older voices unreliable: F3 must not depend on it
)))
|Maturity and feasibility|Commercially available; supplied by the course; documented SDK.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Pepper unavailable: the pocket guide runs seated practice, voice only; the activity coordinator leads as today.
|Benefits|HV-05: a rain-day stand-in that brings back calm and attention, not only exercise (PS-03). HV-06: sessions without a leader for each resident. HV-07: small-group sessions.
|Drawbacks|(((
Strains VT-05: a humanoid adds to the risk of talking down.

Strains VT-02: cameras present.

Mitigations: cameras disabled and visibly covered; no gaze-following; low expressivity; adult voice; leads, then goes quiet.
)))
|Selected or rejected|Selected, indoors only.

== Graph edges ==

enables → F1, F3, F6 (lounge); strains → VT-05, VT-02; addresses → HV-05, HV-06, HV-07; realized-in → RP-01 (§2.2).

= Technology Option - Pocket guide (selected) [TECH-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-02
|Name|Pocket guide (selected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Group reasoning. Off-the-shelf parts: small speaker, accelerometer, Bluetooth Low Energy, battery, large button, pull-cord.
|Category|Wearable / AI-ICT
|Capabilities|(((
Palm-sized speaker. Clips to rollator or wheelchair, or hangs on a lanyard. Same voice as Pepper.

* Plays cues from a prepared script (F2)
* Senses walking, stopped and fall-like impact (accelerometer)
* Hears landmark beacons (TECH-03)
* One large button: pause or continue the voice
* Microphone off outdoors. At the bedside: listens for a few keywords (yes, no, stop, news), on the device; no audio stored or sent
* Pull-cord for help, like the alarm cords in care-home bathrooms (F4)
* Sends alerts over the home's Wi-Fi (F4)
* Docks at the bedside in the evening: speaker for lying practice and audiobooks (F3, F6)
)))
|Limitations|(((
* A speaker is public: passers-by hear it; wind and traffic drown it
* Hearing aids: volume against feedback (Open item O-4)
* Cannot see distress; infers only stops and silence
* Cannot hear the resident outdoors: sadness and "I must go home" are for the companion
* Can be left behind, dropped, or flat
* Alerts need Wi-Fi across the garden
)))
|Maturity and feasibility|Prototype from off-the-shelf parts; feasible within the project.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Silent or flat: the walk goes on as today, with the companion; staff see the degraded state at the start. Alerts fail: the guide asks the companion to call; walk limited to the garden loop.
|Benefits|HV-05: more and longer walks. HV-08: memory invitations from the life story. HV-03: no GPS, no camera; microphone off outdoors.
|Drawbacks|Strains VT-06: memory invitations can be overheard. Mitigation: low volume; no names or avoided topics outdoors.
|Selected or rejected|Selected.

== Graph edges ==

enables → F2, F3 (room), F4, F6 (room); addresses → HV-03, HV-05, HV-08; strains → VT-06; realized-in → RP-02 (§2.2).

= Technology Option - Garden beacons (selected) [TECH-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-03
|Name|Garden beacons (selected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Group reasoning. Bluetooth beacons are standard in museum audio guides and indoor positioning.
|Category|Sensing
|Capabilities|Small battery beacons at landmarks: pond, rose bed, benches, gate. The guide hears the nearest one. Landmark: place-bound cue or memory invitation (F2). Gate: gate alert (F4). No positions stored.
|Limitations|Proximity only, within metres. Misses under interference. Batteries. No landmarks outside the garden.
|Maturity and feasibility|High; standard parts.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Landmark beacon silent: general cues continue. Gate beacon silent: existing door alarms and coded exits still apply. The System adds to exit measures; it never replaces them.
|Benefits|Place-bound cues (HFC-01, HFC-03, HFC-04). A gate event without tracking (VT-02).
|Drawbacks|Strains VT-02 lightly: a momentary location exists. Mitigation: never stored; sent only inside an alert.
|Selected or rejected|Selected.

== Graph edges ==

enables → F2 (place cues), F4 (gate); addresses → HV-04; strains → VT-02.

= Technology Option - Staff notification and session record (selected) [TECH-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-04
|Name|Staff notification and session record (selected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Group reasoning. Care homes already use work phones and nurse-call systems.
|Category|AI/ICT
|Capabilities|Alert to the care worker's phone: resident, trigger, last landmark (F4). Session record on a local server: practice type, minutes, ended early, person called and reason (F5).
|Limitations|A separate app from the nurse-call system; integration likely out of scope. A second channel risks alert fatigue.
|Maturity and feasibility|High; standard messaging and storage.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Alert not acknowledged: re-sent, then escalated to a second worker. Record not stored: session flagged as unrecorded.
|Benefits|HV-04: help arrives. HV-06: called only when needed.
|Drawbacks|Strains VT-02: the record is data about a resident. Kept to four items (UC01-F5-PR5).
|Selected or rejected|Selected.

== Graph edges ==

enables → F4, F5; addresses → HV-04, HV-06; strains → VT-02.

= Technology Option - Listening library and recommender (selected) [TECH-05] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-05
|Name|Listening library and recommender (selected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Group reasoning. Personalised music evidence in HFC-05. Rule-based recommendation chosen for being simple and explainable.
|Category|AI/ICT
|Capabilities|(((
Library: live radio, news bulletins (easy-language source, Open item O-6), audiobooks and short stories, music by era.

Profile: life story, favourites, avoided topics, liked or skipped per item.

Rules:

* Hour: news offered in the morning; calm content in the evening
* State: after a walk, restless, rainy day
* Profile: own era, own region, own interests
* Two options per offer (IDP-03)
* Short stories and familiar books first; for long books, a one-line recap per chapter, prepared in advance
* News on request, any time, in one step

Plays through Pepper (lounge) or the docked pocket guide (room).
)))
|Limitations|Rules capture taste roughly. Profile only as good as the family's input. Streaming and audiobook licences. Quality of the news source.
|Maturity and feasibility|Medium. Rules and streams feasible; licensing to check.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Recommender down: offers the two standing favourites from the profile.
|Benefits|HV-01: choice. HV-08: own content. HV-05: calmer evenings.
|Drawbacks|Strains VT-06 (the profile) and VT-04 (the evening default). Mitigations as in their resolutions. A learned (machine-learning) recommender was considered and not chosen: it needs listening logs (VT-06).
|Selected or rejected|Selected, rule-based.

== Graph edges ==

enables → F6; addresses → HV-01, HV-05, HV-08; strains → VT-04, VT-06.

= Technology Option - GPS tracker (rejected) [TECH-06] =

**Report Format:** §1.3 Technology

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|TECH-06
|Name|GPS tracker (rejected)
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Commercially common for people with dementia. Concerns reviewed in Niemeijer et al. (2010).
|Category|Sensing
|Capabilities|Continuous position, anywhere; geofence alerts.
|Limitations|Continuous route trace.
|Maturity and feasibility|High.
|Failure and fallback behaviour|Not applicable - rejected.
|Benefits|Finds a resident beyond the garden.
|Drawbacks|Violates HV-03; continuous monitoring (HFC-07); strains VT-02 more than any other option.
|Selected or rejected|Rejected. Revisit only for walks beyond the garden, and only if ST-05 requires it.

